id: k8s.probe.readiness
kind: kubernetes
title: Readiness probe
aliases: readiness, readinessProbe
tags: operations, availability, health
questions:
  - how does Kubernetes know a pod can receive traffic?
  - how do you avoid sending traffic to a starting pod?
  - what are the three Kubernetes probes and when is each the right tool?
invariant: A Pod receives Service traffic only while its readiness probe passes; failure removes it from the endpoint list without restarting it.
answer_30s: A readiness probe gates traffic: failing removes the Pod from the Service endpoints, and it comes back automatically once the probe passes again.
answer_2m: |
  Readiness answers "can this replica serve right now?" -- not "is the process
  alive". It must fail when a dependency the request path needs is down; that is
  how a rollout to a broken version stops receiving traffic. Two operational
  details: a Pod removed from endpoints still exists (unlike a failing liveness
  probe, which restarts it), and removal in the data path is not instantaneous,
  so clients still need retries or connection draining.
when_to_use:
  - every workload that receives Service traffic
avoid_when:
  - as a liveness substitute, or with a check that fails on transient dependency slowness
commands:
  - kubectl get pods -o wide => READY column is readiness, not liveness
  - kubectl describe pod api-... => which probe failed, how often, and the last transition
  - kubectl get endpoints api => empty list usually means readiness is failing
minimal_example: |
  readinessProbe:
    httpGet: { path: /healthz/ready, port: 8080 }
    initialDelaySeconds: 1
    periodSeconds: 5
    failureThreshold: 3
failure_modes:
  - bad readiness probe that passes while the app cannot serve
  - probe depends on a flaky dependency, flapping entire replicas out of rotation
  - startup slower than the probe budget, so Pods are never Ready
follow_ups:
  - what is the difference between readiness and liveness?
related:
  - k8s.probe.liveness
  - k8s.probe.startup
  - k8s.deployment.rolling-update
  - k8s.endpoints
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-liveness-readiness-startup-probes/
